"Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap (https://im7.freeforumzone.it/up/72/65/1608312795.pdf)\nciteturn22view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://im7.freeforumzone.it/up/72/65/1608312795.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 817\nL0@P0: Letters\nL1@P0: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1\nL2@P0: 1\nL3@P0: National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan. 2Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for Advanced Studies (SOKENDAI), Okazaki,\nL4@P0: Japan. 3JST, PRESTO, Saitama, Japan. 4Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Graduate School of Science and Engineering, Saitama\nL5@P0: University, Saitama, Japan. 5Department of Botany, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA. 6Institute for Molecular Plant Physiology and Biophysics,\nL6@P0: University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany. 7Department of Biology, Graduate School of Science, Kobe University, Kobe, Japan. 8Graduate School of\nL7@P0: Business Administration, Hitotsubashi University, Kunitachi, Japan. 9Zoology Department, College of Science, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. 10School of Engineering, Utsunomiya University, Utsunomiya, Japan. ✉e-mail: mhasebe@nibb.ac.jp\nL8@P0: The leaves of the carnivorous plant Venus flytrap, Dionaea\nL9@P0: muscipula (Dionaea) close rapidly to capture insect prey. The\nL10@P0: closure response usually requires two successive mechanical\nL11@P0: stimuli to sensory hairs on the leaf blade within approximately\nL12@P0: 30 s (refs. 1–4). An unknown biological system in Dionaea is\nL13@P0: thought to memorize the first stimulus and transduce the\nL14@P0: signal from the sensory hair to the leaf blade2\nL15@P0: . Here, we link\nL16@P0: signal memory to calcium dynamics using transgenic Dionaea\nL17@P0: expressing a Ca2+ sensor. Stimulation of a sensory hair caused\nL18@P0: an increase in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) starting\nL19@P0: in the sensory hair and spreading to the leaf blade. A second\nL20@P0: stimulus increased [Ca2+]cyt to an even higher level, meeting a\nL21@P0: threshold that is correlated to the leaf blade closure. Because\nL22@P0: [Ca2+]cyt gradually decreased after the first stimulus, the\nL23@P0: [Ca2+]cyt increase induced by the second stimulus was insuf\u0002ficient to meet the putative threshold for movement after\nL24@P0: about 30 s. The Ca2+ wave triggered by mechanical stimula\u0002tion moved an order of magnitude faster than that induced by\nL25@P0: wounding in petioles of Arabidopsis thaliana5\nL26@P0:  and Dionaea. The\nL27@P0: capacity for rapid movement has evolved repeatedly in flower\u0002ing plants. This study opens a path to investigate the role of\nL28@P0: Ca2+ in plant movement mechanisms and their evolution.\nL29@P0: Plants have evolved a variety of mechanisms that ‘remember’\nL30@P0: external stimuli as part of numerous processes, including accli\u0002mation to harsh environments, systemic acquired resistance to\nL31@P0: pathogens and vernalization6\nL32@P0: . In the short-term memory system of\nL33@P0: Dionaea (Fig. 1a), the second stimulus can be to any of the leaf ’s six\nL34@P0: sensory hairs (Fig. 1b), regardless of which hair received the first\nL35@P0: stimulus2\nL36@P0: . Previous studies showed that mechanical stimulation of a\nL37@P0: sensory hair generates action potentials that propagate to both lobes\nL38@P0: of the leaf7,8\nL39@P0: . Other studies have shown that leaf closure is blocked\nL40@P0: by Ca2+ channel inhibitors that should inhibit increase of the cyto\u0002solic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) of leaf cells, emphasizing the\nL41@P0: importance of Ca2+ in the response9–11. On the basis of these results,\nL42@P0: the mechanism of Dionaea memory has been proposed to be as fol\u0002lows10,12: (1) [Ca2+]cyt increases during excitation; (2) [Ca2+]cyt or the\nL43@P0: concentration of some Ca2+-activated regulatory molecules must\nL44@P0: reach a threshold for movement; (3) a single action potential is not\nL45@P0: enough to increase [Ca2+]cyt to the threshold and at least two action\nL46@P0: potentials are necessary to trigger movement; (4) [Ca2+]cyt decreases\nL47@P0: after the first stimulus and after 30 s does not reach the threshold\nL48@P0: even with a second stimulus. This hypothesis has not been directly\nL49@P0: tested because of the lack of a method for spatiotemporal calcium\nL50@P0: monitoring in Dionaea.\nL51@P0: Since transformation of Dionaea has not been reported, to\nL52@P0: observe spatiotemporal cytosolic [Ca2+] changes in Dionaea,\nL53@P0: we set out to transform excised leaves with Agrobacterium car\u0002rying a construct for constitutive expression of sGFP (refs. 13–15;\nL54@P0: pSB111U2-sGFP; Supplementary Fig. 1a). On the basis of the num\u0002ber of leaves expressing sGFP, etiolated leaves had a significantly\nL55@P0: higher transformation rate (97% (n=96)) than did non-etiolated\nL56@P0: leaves (46% (n=91)) (two-tail P=4.8×10–12 by Fisher’s exact\nL57@P0: test). Furthermore, shoot regeneration rates from etiolated leaves\nL58@P0: were significantly higher under continuous dark conditions (55%\nL59@P0: (n=60)) than under continuous light conditions (27% (n=60))\nL60@P0: (two-tail P=0.0028 by Fisher’s exact test). From 77 excised etiolated\nL61@P0: leaves, we obtained seven transgenic plants that constitutively and\nL62@P0: ubiquitously expressed sGFP (Supplementary Fig. 2).\nL63@P0: Using the same procedures, we next produced plants trans\u0002formed with a construct for constitutive expression of the calcium\nL64@P0: sensor protein GCaMP6f, which contains a cpEGFP fluorescent\nL65@P0: moiety14,16 (pSB111U2-GCaMP6f; Supplementary Fig. 1b). Five\nL66@P0: transgenic plants were obtained from 245 excised etiolated leaves.\nL67@P0: When non-excised leaves of GCaMP6f-transgenic Dionaea were\nL68@P0: placed on ice, cpEGFP fluorescence increased in both leaf lobes and\nL69@P0: petioles (Fig. 1c), as reported for cold-treated leaves of Arabidopsis\nL70@P0: thaliana17. Cytosolic localization of cpEGFP fluorescence was\nL71@P0: observed in a cut leaf blade (Supplementary Fig. 3).\nL72@P0: We mechanically stimulated a sensory hair of GCaMP6f Dionaea\nL73@P0: and observed that cpEGFP fluorescence increased within 0.02 s at\nL74@P0: the base of the hair (Fig. 2a), where the sensory cells that gener\u0002ate action potentials are located7\nL75@P0: . Over time, fluorescence intensity\nL76@P0: continued to increase in the sensory hair (Fig. 2a) and fluorescence\nL77@P0: spread radially from the sensory hair to the surrounding leaf tissue\nL78@P0: (Fig. 2c,d, Extended Data Fig. 1 and Supplementary Videos 1 and 2).\nL79@P0: There are non-pigmented glands and pigmented digestive glands on\nL80@P0: the leaf blade2\nL81@P0: . The increase of fluorescence intensity was detected\nL82@P0: in the former glands but not in the latter. Fluorescence also propa\u0002gated from one lobe of the leaf to the other (Fig. 2e,f left, Extended\nL83@P0: Data Fig. 2 and Supplementary Video 3). In the abaxial view, fluo\u0002rescence was not detected in the midrib (Fig. 2f right). This suggests\nL84@P0: that fluorescence is propagated in the epidermis and/or parenchyma\nL85@P0: adaxial to the midrib but not in the midrib and tissue surrounding\nL86@P0: the midrib, both of which are connected to the petiole (Fig. 2f,g).\nL87@P0: Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized\nL88@P0: in transgenic Venus flytrap\nL89@P0: Hiraku Suda  1,2, Hiroaki Mano  1,2,3, Masatsugu Toyota  4,5, Kenji Fukushima  1,2,6, Tetsuro Mimura7,\nL90@P0: Izuo Tsutsui8, Rainer Hedrich  6,9, Yosuke Tamada  1,2,10 and Mitsuyasu Hasebe  1,2 ✉\nL91@P0-1: Nature Plants | www.nature.com/natureplantsLetters Nature Plants\nL92@P1: This is consistent with the observation that fluorescence did not\nL93@P1: propagate into the petiole (Fig. 2f).\nL94@P1: We could not localize fluorescence to specific cells because of\nL95@P1: the difficulty of observing intact cells deep within the leaf and the\nL96@P1: need to avoid wound-induced fluorescence that would be caused\nL97@P1: by physical dissection (Supplementary Fig. 3). The fluorescence\nL98@P1: signal extended laterally as far as the area where secondary leaf\nL99@P1: veins, which run perpendicularly from a primary vein in the midrib\nL100@P1: toward the leaf edges, begin to branch and merge with neighbour\u0002ing veins (Fig. 2h). This was the furthest lateral spread observed on\nL101@P1: both the adaxial and abaxial sides of the leaf (Fig. 2f).\nL102@P1: The average propagation velocity in the medial direction\nL103@P1: (53±8.2mms–1 (mean±s.e.m., n=8 leaves)) was faster than that in\nL104@P1: the lateral direction (20±6.8mms–1 (mean±s.e.m., n=8 leaves)).\nL105@P1: Average velocities in the proximal and distal directions were similar\nL106@P1: to that in the lateral direction (23±3.2mms–1 (mean±s.e.m., n=8\nL107@P1: leaves) and 21±3.2mms–1 (mean±s.e.m., n=8 leaves), respec\u0002tively) (Fig. 2d and Extended Data Fig. 3a). The velocity did not\nL108@P1: change when the fluorescence crossed a leaf vein or the midrib\nL109@P1: (Supplementary Video 3). The Ca2+ wave velocities resulting from\nL110@P1: stimulation of a sensory hair and those caused by wounding to the\nL111@P1: leaf blade were not distinguished (Extended Data Figs. 3c and 4,\nL112@P1: Supplementary Table 1 and Supplementary Video 4). It should be\nL113@P1: noted that similar action potentials are produced in response to\nL114@P1: mechanical stimulation and wounding18.\nL115@P1: Fluorescence of unstimulated sensory hairs increased when\nL116@P1: the wave front reached them (Fig. 2c and Supplementary Video\nL117@P1: 1). This is consistent with the observation that leaf closure can be\nL118@P1: induced with a second stimulus to any sensory hair, not only the\nL119@P1: one that received the first stimulus. It is noteworthy that the Ca2+\nL120@P1: wave velocities resulting from stimulation of a sensory hair as well\nL121@P1: as those caused by wounding to the leaf blade are approximately\nL122@P1: 50 to 20 times faster than those caused by wounding to petioles in\nL123@P1: A. thaliana (1.089mms–1) (ref. 5\nL124@P1: ) and in Dionaea (1.2±0.17mms–1\nL125@P1: (mean±s.e.m., n=5 leaves)) (Extended Data Figs. 3d and 5 and\nL126@P1: Supplementary Video 5).\nL127@P1: We next observed Ca2+ dynamics following a second stimulus.\nL128@P1: The second stimulus further increased the signal intensity of the\nL129@P1: stimulated sensory hair (Fig. 2b), then propagated radially to mostly\nL130@P1: the same area as the first stimulus did and the leaf closed (Fig. 2c,\nL131@P1: Extended Data Fig. 6 and Supplementary Video 1). Average propa\u0002gation velocities for the second stimulus were approximately two to\nL132@P1: three times faster than those for the first stimulus in all four direc\u0002tions (Fig. 2c, Extended Data Fig. 3b, Supplementary Table 1 and\nL133@P1: Supplementary Video 1), suggesting that cells with an increased\nL134@P1: [Ca2+]cyt caused by the first stimulus are more sensitive to a second\nL135@P1: stimulus. The leaf area with increased fluorescence was indistin\u0002guishable from the area that changed tissue curvature during the\nL136@P1: leaf closure in a previous study19.\nL137@P1: These results and the Ca2+ requirement of the snap movement9–11\nL138@P1: are concordant with the previous proposal that [Ca2+]cyt increases\nL139@P1: during excitation and is involved in triggering movement10. However,\nL140@P1: to clarify the causality between [Ca2+]cyt and movement, future\nL141@P1: genetic studies are necessary. The anisotropic differences in propaga\u0002tion velocities of the [Ca2+]cyt increase and the acceleration of propa\u0002gation velocities in response to the second stimulus (Extended Data\nL142@P1: Fig. 3 and Supplementary Table 1) are qualitatively in agreement\nL143@P1: with the behaviour of leaf action potentials8\nL144@P1: . However, the action\nL145@P1: potentials propagate at 60–170mms–1 after the first stimulus and\nL146@P1: 250mms–1 after the second stimulus8,20, much faster than the propa\u0002gation of [Ca2+]cyt changes (20–53mms–1 after the first stimulus,\nL147@P1: 51–102mms–1 after the second stimulus; Supplementary Table 1).\nL148@P1: Furthermore, lateral movement of the [Ca2+]cyt wave stops once it\nL149@P1: reached the area where the secondary veins branch (Fig. 2f), while\nL150@P1: action potentials continue beyond that boundary to reach the mar\u0002gin of the leaf blade8\nL151@P1: . These results suggest that propagation of the\nL152@P1: [Ca2+]cyt wave is not directly linked to that of the action potentials.\nL153@P1: Since different experimental setups result in variable velocities of\nL154@P1: action potentials21, a measurement of action potentials in GCaMP6f\nL155@P1: Dionaea together with [Ca2+]cyt changes with an intact leaf is neces\u0002sary to further investigate the relationship between [Ca2+]cyt wave and\nL156@P1: action potential, although it is not possible at this stage in Dionaea.\nL157@P1: To investigate whether the need for two stimuli can be explained\nL158@P1: by there being a [Ca2+]cyt threshold for movement10, we quantitated\nL159@P1: GCaMP6f fluorescence intensity in the lateral and central areas of\nL160@P1: leaf blades (Fig. 2h) every 0.02 s after a mechanical stimulus to a sen\u0002sory hair (Fig. 3a,b). We normalized fluorescence intensity at time\nL161@P1: t to the maximum intensity after the first stimulus, to yield relative\nL162@P1: intensity Ft\nL163@P1:  (Fig. 3a and Extended Data Fig. 7). The first stimulus\nL164@P1: triggered an elevation in fluorescence intensity, which then grad\u0002ually decreased in both areas of the leaf (Fig. 3a,b). Fluorescence\nL165@P1: increased additively in response to the second stimulus in both\nL166@P1: areas and then the leaf closed. The maximum fluorescence intensity\nL167@P1: after the second stimulus was higher than that reached after the first\nL168@P1: stimulus. Fluorescence intensities after the start of leaf movement\nL169@P1: could not be accurately measured because of the rapid movement.\nL170@P1: Because movement is triggered by the second stimulus, we mea\u0002sured the residual Ft\nL171@P1:  immediately before the second stimulus (Fres)\nL172@P1: and the maximum Ft\nL173@P1:  just after the second stimulus (Fmax) (Fig. 3a).\nL174@P1: Logistic regression analysis showed that Fres in both lateral and cen\u0002tral areas are significantly correlated with leaf closure (Fig. 3c and\nL175@P1: Table 1). However, Fmax in the lateral area, but not in the central\nL176@P1: area, are significantly correlated with leaf closure. It is possible that\nL177@P1: [Ca2+]cyt exceeded the detection range of GCaMP6f in the central\nL178@P1: area after the second stimulus, because fluorescence intensity was\nL179@P1: higher in the central area than in the lateral area (Extended Data\nL180@P1: Fig. 7). The predicted 95% confidence intervals (CI) of putative\nL181@P1: threshold Fres, where leaf closure is triggered with 50% probability,\nL182@P1: L\nL183@P1: M\nL184@P1: D\nL185@P1: P\nL186@P1: High\nL187@P1: Low[Ca2+]cyt\nL188@P1: b\nL189@P1: c\nL190@P1: a\nL191@P1: Fig. 1 | Cold stimulation induces a [Ca2+]cyt increase in a Dionaea trap\nL192@P1: leaf. a, A Dionaea plant growing in its natural habitat, North Carolina,\nL193@P1: United States. Leaves form a rosette and the bilobed leaf blade of each leaf\nL194@P1: forms a trap. b, Six sensory hairs (arrows) of a Dionaea leaf, one of which\nL195@P1: is magnified in the inset. L, lateral direction; M, medial direction; D, distal\nL196@P1: direction; P, proximal direction. Similar results were obtained from three\nL197@P1: leaves of three biologically independent plants. c, GCaMP6f fluorescence\nL198@P1: images of a leaf blade before (left) and 2 min after (right) a cold stimulus.\nL199@P1: The abaxial side of a petiole and right half of a leaf blade (a right leaf lobe)\nL200@P1: were attached to an ice block and cpEGFP signals were detected in cells\nL201@P1: attached to the ice. The left leaf lobe (outlined by dashed line) did not\nL202@P1: touch the ice block and the cpEGFP signal was not detected. Scale bars,\nL203@P1: 1 cm in a; 1 mm in b and c; 100 μm in the inset of b.\nL204@P1-2: Nature Plants | www.nature.com/natureplantsNature Plants Letters\nL205@P2: were 0.3738–0.6598 and 0.2599–0.4999 in the lateral and central\nL206@P2: areas, respectively (Table 1). The 95% CI of putative threshold Fmax\nL207@P2: was 1.226–1.422 in the lateral area. A likelihood ratio test rejected\nL208@P2: the assumption that leaf closure does not depend on [Ca2+]cyt\nL209@P2: (Table 1). These results indicate that there are putative [Ca2+]cyt\nL210@P2: thresholds for immediately before and after the second stimulus\nL211@P2: that should be met for movement to occur. Since the predicted 95%\nL212@P2: CI of putative threshold Fmax (1.226–1.422) is higher than that for\nL213@P2: F1 (1.000), a single stimulus cannot raise [Ca2+]cyt over the putative\nL214@P2: threshold Fmax and a second stimulus is necessary to meet the puta\u0002tive threshold for movement, at least in the lateral area.\nL215@P2: Next, we examined whether a second stimulus could raise [Ca2+]cyt\nL216@P2: to the threshold after the memory limit time of 30s after the first stim\u0002ulus2,3\nL217@P2: . We measured Ft every 0.02s after the time of F1 in the absence\nL218@P2: of a second stimulus (Fig. 3d,e). [Ca2+]cyt decreased with time in both\nL219@P2: lateral and central areas of the leaf and decay curves were well fit into\nL220@P2: the two-phase exponential curves with lower Akaike’s Information\nL221@P2: Criterion in comparison to other simpler models (Supplementary\nL222@P2: Fig. 4). Fres and Fmax in the separate experiments with the second\nL223@P2: stimulus were plotted on the same graph with showing whether\nL224@P2: the leaf closed or not at given time (Fig. 3d,e). Leaf closure depends\nL225@P2: on the time elapsed after F1 is reached. Logistic regression analysis\nL226@P2: High\nL227@P2: First stimulus\nL228@P2: Second stimulus\nL229@P2: Maximum intensity after\nL230@P2: first stimulus\nL231@P2: Maximum intensity after\nL232@P2: second stimulus\nL233@P2: Start of\nL234@P2: leaf movement\nL235@P2: Lateral area: green\nL236@P2: Central area: purple\nL237@P2: 0.00 s a\nL238@P2: f g h\nL239@P2: d e\nL240@P2: b\nL241@P2: c\nL242@P2: 0.02 s 0.04 s 0.06 s 0.08 s 0.62 s 1.14 s\nL243@P2: 0.00 s\nL244@P2: 0.00 s\nL245@P2: 0.02 s 0.04 s 0.06 s 0.08 s\nL246@P2: 0.08 s 0.16 s 0.24 s 0.32 s 0.40 s 1.06 s\nL247@P2: 2.56 s 4.06 s\nL248@P2: Lateral\nL249@P2: Medial\nL250@P2: Distal Proximal\nL251@P2: 5.56 s 5.64 s 5.80 s 6.14 s 9.14 s\nL252@P2: 0.48 s 1.88 s\nL253@P2: [Ca2+]cyt\nL254@P2: Low\nL255@P2: High [Ca2+]cyt\nL256@P2: Low\nL257@P2: Fig. 2 | A [Ca2+]cyt increase propagates from the sensory hair to the leaf blade after mechanical stimulation. a,b, Bright-field (a, leftmost) and fluorescence\nL258@P2: (others) images of a sensory hair stimulated by a needle at the indicated position by arrow heads. Seconds (s) from the first (a) or second (b) stimulus is\nL259@P2: indicated. Rightmost fluorescence images are the frames with the maximum intensity scored after the stimulus. Maximum intensity was measured across\nL260@P2: the whole pictured area. The second stimulus was applied 13.08 s after the first stimulus. Similar results were obtained from three leaves of two biologically\nL261@P2: independent plants. c, Fluorescence images of a GCaMP6f Dionaea leaf blade after a sensory hair was stimulated with a needle (yellow arrow). Frames were\nL262@P2: extracted in pseudocolour from those shown in Supplementary Video 1. Seconds (s) after the first stimulus are indicated. White arrows indicate sensory hairs\nL263@P2: with fluorescence despite not having been mechanical stimulated. The leaf closed after the second stimulus. Maximum intensity was measured across the\nL264@P2: whole pictured area. d,e, Propagation of the [Ca2+]cyt increase following the first mechanical stimulation to a sensory hair, indicated on bright-field images\nL265@P2: of the leaf shown in c (d) and in Extended Data Fig. 2 (e). Boundaries of the propagated area are shown every 0.02 s (white dashed line) and 0.08 s (white\nL266@P2: solid line). The boundary of the maximum propagated area is shown by a white dash-dotted line, 1.06 s (d) and 0.60 s (e) after application of the mechanical\nL267@P2: stimulus, respectively. f, Bright-field (top) and fluorescence (bottom) images of the adaxial (left and middle) and abaxial (right) sides of leaves showing the\nL268@P2: frame at which the maximum intensity after the mechanical stimulation was scored. Intensity was measured across the whole pictured area. White and red\nL269@P2: lines indicate outlines of a leaf blade and petiole, respectively. Magenta lines indicate leaf veins around the lateral boundary of fluorescence. g, Transverse\nL270@P2: section of tissue around the midrib. Black arrow heads indicate borders between fluorescent and non-fluorescent regions in the abaxial surface. The midrib is\nL271@P2: indicated by a bracket. Similar results were obtained from three leaves of three biologically independent plants. h, Bright-field images of a cleared leaf blade.\nL272@P2: Lateral and central areas used in the logistic regression analysis are indicated in green and magenta in the upper image. Secondary veins are indicated in the\nL273@P2: lower image (yellow lines). Scale bars, 200 μm in a and b; 1 mm in c–f and h; 100 μm in g.\nL274@P2-3: Nature Plants | www.nature.com/natureplantsLetters Nature Plants\nL275@P3: showed that the putative 50% leaf closure threshold for the duration\nL276@P3: of the F1 signal was 30.88s with 95% CI 21.4–45.42s (Fig. 3f and\nL277@P3: Table 1), which concurs with previous studies2,3\nL278@P3: . The means of F30.88\nL279@P3: in the lateral and central areas were 0.4631±0.07214 (mean±s.e.m.,\nL280@P3: n=6 leaves) (Fig. 3d) and 0.2638±0.07758 (mean±s.e.m., n=6\nL281@P3: leaves) (Fig. 3e), respectively, which are within the ranges of 95% CIs\nL282@P3: of putative Fres thresholds (Table 1). These results indicate that the\nL283@P3: time course of [Ca2+]cyt decay correlates with the duration of the sig\u0002nal produced by the first stimulus and that the 30-s length of mem\u0002ory is explained by the decay of [Ca2+]cyt.\nL284@P3: We also investigated whether the third stimulus can increase\nL285@P3: [Ca2+]cyt over the putative threshold Fmax and trigger movement,\nL286@P3: when the second stimulus does not trigger movement. After the\nL287@P3: third stimulus, Ft\nL288@P3:  at the lateral area reached the 95% CI of the\nL289@P3: putative threshold and the leaf closed (Extended Data Fig. 8 and\nL290@P3: Supplementary Fig. 5 and Supplementary Video 6). On the other\nL291@P3: hand, even when Ft\nL292@P3:  at central area reached the 95% CI of the\nL293@P3: putative threshold, the leaf did not close (Supplementary Fig. 5\nL294@P3: and Extended Data Fig. 8). These results are concordant with the\nL295@P3: result that Fmax at lateral area significantly correlates to the move\u0002ment but Fmax at central area does not (Table 1).\nL296@P3: To further investigate relationships between the putative [Ca2+]cyt\nL297@P3: threshold and movement, two experiments were used. We found\nL298@P3: that Dionaea leaves close with no mechanical stimuli within 1min,\nL299@P3: Lateral area\nL300@P3: F1 Ft\nL301@P3: Fres\nL302@P3: 2nd\nL303@P3: 2nd\nL304@P3: Leaf closed Leaf closed\nL305@P3: 1 1 st st\nL306@P3: Fmax\nL307@P3: Lateral area Lateral area\nL308@P3: Lateral area\nL309@P3: Green: Fmax\nL310@P3: Magenta: Fres\nL311@P3: Green: Fmax\nL312@P3: Magenta: Fres\nL313@P3: Central area\nL314@P3: Central area Central area\nL315@P3: Central area\nL316@P3: Standardized fluorescence inten